1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:01,970
Okay, next section is breast cancer.

2
00:00:02,110 --> 00:00:06,030
This is the longest section and particularly the highest yield section in this chapter,

3
00:00:06,230 --> 00:00:10,830
so we're going to spend a little bit more time doing this section than we have on the prior sections.

4
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:16,670
Before we begin, let's remind ourselves that the breast cancer is the most common carcinoma in women by incidence,

5
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:22,270
and of course we're excluding skin, so the most common carcinoma in women by incidence excluding skin,

6
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:29,390
and it's the second most common cause of cancer mortality, the first most common cause being lung cancer.

7
00:00:29,390 --> 00:00:35,430
The risk factors are mostly related to estrogen exposure, and when you look at this list that I placed here in your text,

8
00:00:35,810 --> 00:00:38,050
you'll see that that becomes very quickly evident.

9
00:00:38,510 --> 00:00:42,550
So for example, female gender is the first risk factor. That's the number one risk factor.

10
00:00:42,930 --> 00:00:49,110
When you think about breast cancer, for every 100 cases, 99 will occur in a female, and only one will occur in a male,

11
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:52,690
and of course that has to do with estrogen. Age is a risk factor.

12
00:00:53,170 --> 00:00:58,590
Generally speaking, the classic patient who has breast cancer is a postmenopausal female,

13
00:00:58,590 --> 00:01:05,870
and you can see breast cancer in younger patients, but it begins to suggest that there may be a genetic abnormality

14
00:01:05,870 --> 00:01:10,790
that's driving that breast cancer, but classically speaking, just you really think about breast cancer

15
00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:19,710
as a disease of postmenopausal females. Early menarche and late menopause would be a sign of increased exposure to estrogen,

16
00:01:19,710 --> 00:01:24,850
and if the breast sees increased exposure to estrogen, there's going to be an increased risk for breast cancer.

17
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:32,130
Obesity, remember that adipose tissue has the ability to convert androgen to estrone, which is a type of estrogen,

18
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:35,350
and that would therefore increase the risk for breast cancer.

19
00:01:36,290 --> 00:01:45,290
Atypical hyperplasia, this refers to a patient having a prior biopsy in which atypical hyperplasia was seen.

20
00:01:45,690 --> 00:01:49,070
Remember that when we talked about atypical hyperplasia in the prior section,

21
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:57,370
we mentioned that when patients have atypical hyperplasia on a biopsy, that increases their risk five times relative to the normal population

22
00:01:57,370 --> 00:02:03,830
of developing invasive breast cancer in either breast, and that's what we mean by atypical hyperplasia here.

23
00:02:04,130 --> 00:02:09,270
And of course, if there's atypical hyperplasia, it kind of implies that there must be excess estrogen on board

24
00:02:09,270 --> 00:02:13,990
or some sort of estrogen-related risk factor because that's why the hyperplasia is occurring.

25
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:15,530
It's driven by estrogen often.

26
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:22,630
And then finally, one of the risk factors is family history, in particular, first-degree relative with breast cancer.

27
00:02:22,790 --> 00:02:25,890
And it's important to note here who the first-degree relatives are.

28
00:02:25,970 --> 00:02:29,650
So they include sister, mother, and daughter.

29
00:02:30,590 --> 00:02:31,570
Sister, mother, daughter.

30
00:02:31,670 --> 00:02:33,550
Those are the three first-degree relatives.

31
00:02:34,770 --> 00:02:38,390
Now, the first of the cancers that we're going to talk about is ductal carcinoma in situ.

32
00:02:38,390 --> 00:02:44,950
I think it's best to just take a step back and establish a few basic principles before we actually go into the subtypes of cancer.

33
00:02:45,070 --> 00:02:45,870
So let's do this.

34
00:02:46,270 --> 00:02:52,210
Let's go back and remind ourselves that the functional unit of the breast is something called the terminal duct lobular unit.

35
00:02:52,870 --> 00:02:58,030
So here would represent the terminal duct, and then you're aware that the lobule contains these glands

36
00:02:58,030 --> 00:03:01,670
and that these glands are really the milk-producing glands of the breast.

37
00:03:01,670 --> 00:03:08,050
Now, it's possible when you have cancer that you can get a malignant proliferation of cells within the duct.

38
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:16,790
And when you get a malignant proliferation of cells within the duct, that's called ductal carcinoma in situ.

39
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:22,790
And the key feature here is that these malignant cells are bound by this basement membrane.

40
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:25,030
So that's ductal carcinoma in situ.

41
00:03:25,030 --> 00:03:33,750
If these ductal carcinoma in situ cells walk up the duct and eventually come out to the skin of the nipple,

42
00:03:34,570 --> 00:03:38,790
into the epidermis of the nipple, then that's called Paget's disease of the nipple.

43
00:03:39,370 --> 00:03:45,470
So when you have Paget's disease of the nipple, that is a ductal carcinoma in situ that has walked its way up the duct.

44
00:03:46,010 --> 00:03:47,770
So that's important to note.

45
00:03:48,770 --> 00:03:54,210
If these malignant cells invade through the basement membrane and into the surrounding stroma,

46
00:03:54,210 --> 00:03:57,690
then we call that invasive ductal carcinoma.

47
00:03:58,250 --> 00:04:00,490
So that's another subtype of breast cancer.

48
00:04:00,890 --> 00:04:04,610
It's also possible to have a malignant proliferation of cells within the lobule.

49
00:04:05,230 --> 00:04:09,170
That would be called lobular carcinoma in situ.

50
00:04:10,250 --> 00:04:14,910
And if these malignant cells invade through the basement membrane and into the connective tissue,

51
00:04:15,330 --> 00:04:19,550
then we would call that invasive lobular carcinoma.

52
00:04:19,550 --> 00:04:23,150
So these are really our four main subtypes of breast cancer.

53
00:04:23,150 --> 00:04:30,790
Ductal carcinoma in situ, invasive ductal carcinoma, and then lobular carcinoma in situ, and invasive lobular carcinoma.

54
00:04:31,130 --> 00:04:35,930
And what we're going to do over the next few slides is now provide some more detail of this paradigm.

55
00:04:36,050 --> 00:04:40,290
So this is the global theme that you have to have in your mind as you walk through breast cancer.

56
00:04:41,290 --> 00:04:44,450
Let's go back and begin our discussion with ductal carcinoma in situ.

57
00:04:44,950 --> 00:04:49,370
So as I said, ductal carcinoma in situ is a malignant proliferation of cells in the ducts.

58
00:04:49,370 --> 00:04:51,990
There is no invasion of the basement membrane.

59
00:04:52,630 --> 00:04:57,630
Now, because these cells are simply growing in ducts, you wouldn't normally be able to detect it as a mass.

60
00:04:57,710 --> 00:04:59,230
It usually doesn't produce a mass.

61
00:04:59,690 --> 00:05:03,130
Instead, it's going to be detected as calcification on mammography.

62
00:05:03,650 --> 00:05:07,270
And the idea here is that if you've got this duct, and I'm showing you this duct head-on,

63
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:10,210
and let's say it's loaded with these malignant cancer cells,

64
00:05:10,210 --> 00:05:14,650
there is no blood supply in the duct, within the duct itself.

65
00:05:14,790 --> 00:05:16,250
Remember, the blood supply is out here.

66
00:05:16,810 --> 00:05:21,490
And so these cells, as they grow, especially if they're high-grade and rapidly growing cells,

67
00:05:21,650 --> 00:05:23,450
the center will often die.

68
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:26,510
And when the center dies, you've got dead cells.

69
00:05:26,970 --> 00:05:30,290
And remember that it's possible to get calcification on top of dead cells,

70
00:05:30,350 --> 00:05:32,030
which is called dystrophic calcification.

71
00:05:32,450 --> 00:05:35,790
So you can develop dystrophic calcification on top of these dead cells,

72
00:05:35,790 --> 00:05:39,410
and mammogram can then pick up that calcification.

73
00:05:40,070 --> 00:05:42,750
Now, remember that calcifications are not always malignant.

74
00:05:42,910 --> 00:05:46,450
For example, we talked about two other lesions in this chapter

75
00:05:46,450 --> 00:05:49,430
in which we could see calcifications, but they were not malignant.

76
00:05:49,550 --> 00:05:50,190
They were benign.

77
00:05:50,570 --> 00:05:51,630
So can you recall those?

78
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:53,850
Number one, fat necrosis.

79
00:05:54,950 --> 00:05:57,310
Remember that I said that when a patient gets fat necrosis,

80
00:05:57,390 --> 00:06:00,070
they get saponification of that fat, and it can be calcified.

81
00:06:00,770 --> 00:06:03,170
And number two, in the discussion of fibrocystic changes,

82
00:06:03,170 --> 00:06:06,070
is I talked about one change called sclerosing adenosis

83
00:06:06,070 --> 00:06:09,570
and told you that that is often calcified as well.

84
00:06:09,690 --> 00:06:12,890
And so therefore, that could be detected as calcification on mammogram.

85
00:06:13,370 --> 00:06:15,310
So this is something important to bear in mind.

86
00:06:16,370 --> 00:06:20,310
Now, there are actually multiple different patterns of ductal carcinoma in situ.

87
00:06:20,890 --> 00:06:24,510
However, the one that's super high yield and most important for board exams

88
00:06:24,510 --> 00:06:27,850
and probably for your level of training is the one to be aware of.

89
00:06:27,850 --> 00:06:29,090
It's called the comedotype.

90
00:06:29,830 --> 00:06:32,450
In the comedotype, you have high-grade cells with necrosis

91
00:06:32,450 --> 00:06:34,850
and dystrophic calcification in the center of the ducts

92
00:06:34,850 --> 00:06:36,310
is exactly what I've already drawn.

93
00:06:36,830 --> 00:06:37,710
Here's a picture of that.

94
00:06:37,810 --> 00:06:39,690
This is a duct cut head-on.

95
00:06:39,850 --> 00:06:40,890
So here you've got this duct.

96
00:06:41,230 --> 00:06:44,030
And you can see that this duct is loaded with all these cells.

97
00:06:44,130 --> 00:06:45,110
So there's too many cells.

98
00:06:45,210 --> 00:06:47,410
And then in the center, there's this necrosis.

99
00:06:47,530 --> 00:06:49,710
And the center of that necrosis is calcified.

100
00:06:50,090 --> 00:06:54,110
So this is the classic example of the comedotype of ductal carcinoma in situ.

101
00:06:54,870 --> 00:06:57,250
Now, remember, I told you that if you have DCIS,

102
00:06:57,250 --> 00:07:01,490
for example, in this duct, and then it climbs up its way through the duct

103
00:07:01,490 --> 00:07:05,050
and eventually gets into the skin of the nipple in particular,

104
00:07:05,050 --> 00:07:08,610
we call that Paget's disease of the nipple or Paget's disease of the breast.

105
00:07:08,950 --> 00:07:13,110
So this is ductal carcinoma in situ that extends up the ducts to the skin of the nipple.

106
00:07:14,190 --> 00:07:17,270
It's going to present with nipple ulceration and erythema.

107
00:07:17,730 --> 00:07:21,610
And, of course, if you see a patient and they've got Paget's disease of the nipple,

108
00:07:22,190 --> 00:07:26,050
let's say that you have a patient and they've got this nipple ulceration and erythema,

109
00:07:26,170 --> 00:07:29,370
and you do a biopsy and you prove that there's Paget's disease of the nipple,

110
00:07:29,770 --> 00:07:32,450
that would tell you that there must be a cancer within the breast.

111
00:07:32,910 --> 00:07:36,390
And that's a very important point because if you go back to your gynecologic lectures,

112
00:07:36,510 --> 00:07:40,630
remember that there is also a disorder called extra mammary Paget's disease

113
00:07:40,630 --> 00:07:43,990
where you get malignant cells within the epidermis of the vulva.

114
00:07:44,410 --> 00:07:47,570
And in that particular disorder, there was no underlying carcinoma.

115
00:07:47,890 --> 00:07:51,170
So we do have to make a big deal here about the fact that in Paget's disease,

116
00:07:51,170 --> 00:07:54,130
there will be a cancer someplace in the breast.

117
00:07:54,550 --> 00:07:55,690
So that's important to know.

118
00:07:56,630 --> 00:07:58,610
Here's what Paget's disease of the nipple looks like.

119
00:07:58,930 --> 00:08:04,410
Obviously, you can see this area of ulceration and disturbance of the epithelium

120
00:08:04,410 --> 00:08:05,790
or the epidermis of the nipple.

121
00:08:06,250 --> 00:08:07,970
Here's what it would look like on biopsy.

122
00:08:08,270 --> 00:08:11,450
You normally have your squamous cells, and then in between the squamous cells,

123
00:08:11,810 --> 00:08:15,610
you've got all of these cells with this kind of lighter-colored cytoplasm,

124
00:08:15,610 --> 00:08:23,690
and these are the malignant cells walking their way up the skin or the epidermis of the patient's nipple.

125
00:08:24,310 --> 00:08:26,390
And so, again, this is Paget's disease of the nipple.

126
00:08:27,510 --> 00:08:32,930
Invasive ductal carcinoma is when you actually have the malignant cells go through the basement membrane

127
00:08:32,930 --> 00:08:34,870
and now invade into the connective tissue.

128
00:08:34,870 --> 00:08:39,170
And it classically forms duct-like structures so that when you look at the tumor,

129
00:08:39,510 --> 00:08:42,690
you'll actually see these round duct-like structures.

130
00:08:43,290 --> 00:08:45,770
Now, this is the most common type of invasive carcinoma.

131
00:08:46,170 --> 00:08:50,990
It presents as a mass, and that mass can be detected by physical exam or mammography.

132
00:08:51,490 --> 00:08:54,810
Physical exam will usually detect a mass that's about two centimeters or greater,

133
00:08:55,230 --> 00:08:58,910
whereas mammography can pick up a mass at about one centimeter or greater.

134
00:08:58,910 --> 00:09:05,270
And so mammography is a very good test because it allows us to detect breast cancer at a much earlier stage.

135
00:09:05,790 --> 00:09:10,990
One of the important things to recognize concerning breast cancer is that as the tumor begins to spread through the breast,

136
00:09:11,330 --> 00:09:15,010
it can result in dimpling of the skin or retraction of the nipple.

137
00:09:15,350 --> 00:09:18,690
Now, let's remember that retraction of the nipple is not always due to cancer.

138
00:09:19,110 --> 00:09:21,610
It can be due to inflammatory conditions as well.

139
00:09:21,910 --> 00:09:27,890
However, in this particular case, it is a consequence of cancer, so just something to be aware of.

140
00:09:27,890 --> 00:09:31,950
Now, when you're worried about cancer in the breast, if you have a mass and you're particularly worried,

141
00:09:32,010 --> 00:09:33,010
you're going to do a biopsy.

142
00:09:33,470 --> 00:09:39,610
And when you have invasive ductal carcinoma, you're going to see duct-like structures in a desmoplastic stroma.

143
00:09:40,250 --> 00:09:45,070
And what we mean by desmoplastic stroma is that you have connective tissue growing with the tumor,

144
00:09:45,470 --> 00:09:51,850
and that connective tissue is there to support the tumor and provide its structural support, stromal support, we could say.

145
00:09:52,310 --> 00:09:54,250
And that's called desmoplastic stroma.

146
00:09:54,250 --> 00:09:58,770
Now, there are some special subtypes of invasive ductal carcinoma to be familiar with.

147
00:09:59,290 --> 00:10:04,190
The most common circumstance is that you have your everyday invasive ductal carcinoma.

148
00:10:04,530 --> 00:10:05,790
But there are four subtypes.

149
00:10:05,890 --> 00:10:08,370
They're all high yield, and I want to talk about each of these individually.

150
00:10:08,850 --> 00:10:10,650
So the first is called tubular carcinoma.

151
00:10:10,770 --> 00:10:15,350
And the important thing about tubular carcinoma is that the cancer produces tubules,

152
00:10:16,410 --> 00:10:18,010
little round circular spaces.

153
00:10:18,010 --> 00:10:22,710
And the little round circular spaces look very much like normal breasts.

154
00:10:22,890 --> 00:10:26,530
Sometimes it's difficult to distinguish this from normal.

155
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:31,270
And there's a few features that will help you to determine that this is actually malignant.

156
00:10:31,710 --> 00:10:34,010
Number one, you'll see a desmoplastic stroma.

157
00:10:34,350 --> 00:10:38,750
So you can clearly see here that there's all this kind of blue-gray connective tissue in between,

158
00:10:39,610 --> 00:10:41,650
and that is the desmoplastic stroma.

159
00:10:41,650 --> 00:10:46,190
And number two, if I were to test these cells to see if there was a second cell type,

160
00:10:46,490 --> 00:10:47,950
the second cell type would be absent.

161
00:10:48,330 --> 00:10:52,270
Let's go back and remind ourselves that the normal ducts and lobules of the breast,

162
00:10:52,510 --> 00:10:54,130
they normally have two cell types.

163
00:10:54,230 --> 00:10:58,230
They have an epithelial cell, and then underneath that, they have a myoepithelial cell.

164
00:10:58,630 --> 00:11:01,710
However, when you look at cancer, cancer would not have two cell types.

165
00:11:01,810 --> 00:11:02,490
It's not normal.

166
00:11:02,890 --> 00:11:04,610
So you would only see one cell type here.

167
00:11:04,910 --> 00:11:06,730
And so if I didn't see a second cell type,

168
00:11:07,030 --> 00:11:10,010
that would also prove to me that I'm dealing with a tubular carcinoma.

169
00:11:10,010 --> 00:11:14,950
Now, one important thing about tubular carcinoma is that it has a very good prognosis.

170
00:11:15,230 --> 00:11:16,930
It has a particularly good prognosis.

171
00:11:17,670 --> 00:11:22,810
The second type of special invasive ductal carcinoma is called mucinous carcinoma.

172
00:11:23,090 --> 00:11:27,050
A key feature is that you have malignant cells floating in pools of mucus.

173
00:11:27,430 --> 00:11:29,230
And so you can see these are the malignant cells.

174
00:11:29,330 --> 00:11:30,070
These are not normal.

175
00:11:30,390 --> 00:11:33,470
And look at all of the mucus back here that they're floating in.

176
00:11:33,770 --> 00:11:37,670
So the presence of malignant cells in mucus is called mucinous carcinoma.

177
00:11:37,670 --> 00:11:40,210
There are two important things to know about this subtype.

178
00:11:40,570 --> 00:11:45,270
Number one, it has an excellent prognosis, just as tubular carcinoma has an excellent prognosis.

179
00:11:45,670 --> 00:11:48,950
Perhaps one way to think about it would be that the tumor cells are all stuck in mucus,

180
00:11:49,030 --> 00:11:50,070
so they can't go anywhere.

181
00:11:50,670 --> 00:11:52,990
Now, number two, this usually occurs in elderly women.

182
00:11:53,150 --> 00:11:56,690
So it's seen in, and so it's particularly seen in elderly women.

183
00:11:57,250 --> 00:12:00,910
The third subtype of, special subtype of breast cancer that I want to talk about

184
00:12:00,910 --> 00:12:02,630
is called inflammatory carcinoma.

185
00:12:02,630 --> 00:12:08,350
Now, when a patient has inflammatory carcinoma, they present with a breast that's highly erythematous

186
00:12:08,350 --> 00:12:09,390
and swollen.

187
00:12:09,850 --> 00:12:12,490
So it looks like the patient has acute mastitis.

188
00:12:12,950 --> 00:12:17,730
However, you put the patient on antibiotics, or perhaps the patient may not be feeding,

189
00:12:18,130 --> 00:12:19,510
but let's say the patient is feeding.

190
00:12:19,630 --> 00:12:22,170
You put the patient on antibiotics, and it doesn't really resolve.

191
00:12:22,590 --> 00:12:26,310
Now you become more concerned that perhaps it's not an infection, but it could be,

192
00:12:26,410 --> 00:12:28,710
for example, something called inflammatory carcinoma.

193
00:12:28,710 --> 00:12:32,750
So then you do a biopsy, and when you do a biopsy, you find the characteristic feature,

194
00:12:33,150 --> 00:12:37,550
which is that this type of tumor is characterized by the presence of cancer within the dermal

195
00:12:37,550 --> 00:12:39,130
lymphatics, as I've circled here.

196
00:12:40,270 --> 00:12:44,570
When you have cancer in the dermal lymphatics, the cancer blocks the dermal lymphatics and

197
00:12:44,570 --> 00:12:46,310
decreases the drainage of the breast.

198
00:12:46,650 --> 00:12:50,890
That then results in a swollen erythematous breast, and this is called inflammatory carcinoma

199
00:12:50,890 --> 00:12:51,470
of the breast.

200
00:12:51,930 --> 00:12:54,250
Now, a couple important things about this type of cancer.

201
00:12:54,250 --> 00:12:59,610
Now, number one, one very high-yield point to be aware of is the fact that it is a clinical

202
00:12:59,610 --> 00:13:01,530
pathologic entity.

203
00:13:02,010 --> 00:13:07,110
You need to see inflammatory changes in the breast clinically, and then you must prove that there

204
00:13:07,110 --> 00:13:09,530
is tumor in dermal lymphatics pathologically.

205
00:13:09,970 --> 00:13:12,710
So both of these pieces are essential for this diagnosis.

206
00:13:13,450 --> 00:13:14,350
That's the first point.

207
00:13:14,670 --> 00:13:20,270
The second point is that you should be aware that this has a very poor prognosis, and I

208
00:13:20,270 --> 00:13:24,290
think that's easy to understand because the tumor is already sitting in the dermal lymphatic

209
00:13:24,290 --> 00:13:28,410
spaces and now has access to the lymph nodes and potentially the rest of the body.

210
00:13:29,070 --> 00:13:32,450
And the third point that I want to make about this tumor is to remember to always have it

211
00:13:32,450 --> 00:13:35,830
in the back of your mind when you see a patient that you think has acute mastitis.

212
00:13:36,350 --> 00:13:40,170
So especially on an examination, they can tell you that there's a woman and she's feeding,

213
00:13:40,650 --> 00:13:45,810
and you kind of make a diagnosis of acute mastitis, but that after the antibiotics, the disease

214
00:13:45,810 --> 00:13:47,090
process does not resolve.

215
00:13:47,090 --> 00:13:48,630
And that you need to be careful.

216
00:13:48,790 --> 00:13:52,550
Whenever you have acute mastitis, you need to make sure that you're not dealing with an

217
00:13:52,550 --> 00:13:53,730
inflammatory carcinoma.

218
00:13:54,110 --> 00:13:58,630
So for example, if you have a woman with acute mastitis and you give antibiotics, it's important

219
00:13:58,630 --> 00:14:03,230
to see that woman five or ten days later to ensure that the antibiotics took care of the

220
00:14:03,230 --> 00:14:07,790
problem and that you didn't miss, for example, an inflammatory carcinoma of the breast.

221
00:14:08,250 --> 00:14:10,030
Again, this has a very poor prognosis.

222
00:14:10,610 --> 00:14:15,030
The fourth special subtype, which I don't have a picture of, but it is in your text and

223
00:14:15,030 --> 00:14:17,190
it is high yield, it's called medullary carcinoma.

224
00:14:17,630 --> 00:14:22,390
The special thing about medullary carcinoma is that you have high grade malignant tumor

225
00:14:22,390 --> 00:14:27,170
cells with a bunch of inflammatory cells in the background, particularly lymphocytes

226
00:14:27,170 --> 00:14:28,450
and plasma cells.

227
00:14:29,070 --> 00:14:33,290
Now what's important about this subtype is that there's an increased propensity for this

228
00:14:33,290 --> 00:14:37,130
subtype in patients with BRCA1 mutations, and that's high yield.

229
00:14:37,130 --> 00:14:41,950
So again, in medullary carcinoma, in which you have high grade malignant cells in an inflammatory

230
00:14:41,950 --> 00:14:47,850
background, there's an increased propensity for that subtype of cancer to arise in patients

231
00:14:47,850 --> 00:14:51,190
with BRCA1 mutations, and again, that is high yield.

232
00:14:52,510 --> 00:14:56,750
Now, after we've completed the ductal types of cancers, we then go to the lobular types

233
00:14:56,750 --> 00:15:00,650
of cancers, and let's remind ourselves that I said that if this is the terminal duct and

234
00:15:00,650 --> 00:15:04,810
this is the lobular unit, and you've got all of these little globule, all of these little

235
00:15:04,810 --> 00:15:09,610
glands within the lobule, if you get a malignant proliferation of cells within the lobule, then

236
00:15:09,610 --> 00:15:11,850
we call that lobular carcinoma in situ.

237
00:15:12,450 --> 00:15:17,950
And if you get a malignant proliferation of cells that invades, then we call that invasive

238
00:15:17,950 --> 00:15:19,490
lobular carcinoma.

239
00:15:21,130 --> 00:15:25,450
LCIS, or lobular carcinoma in situ, is a malignant proliferation of cells in the lobules.

240
00:15:25,810 --> 00:15:28,130
Of course, there is no invasion of the basement membrane.

241
00:15:28,710 --> 00:15:32,430
What's interesting about this entity is that it's usually detected incidentally.

242
00:15:32,430 --> 00:15:34,750
What that means is that you find it by accident.

243
00:15:35,210 --> 00:15:36,490
It doesn't produce a mass.

244
00:15:36,870 --> 00:15:38,390
It doesn't produce calcifications.

245
00:15:38,850 --> 00:15:40,990
So you biopsy the breast for one reason.

246
00:15:41,310 --> 00:15:45,670
Let's say there is a mass or a calcification that you're worried about, and then it so happens

247
00:15:45,670 --> 00:15:50,750
that by chance, or incidentally, you discover that the patient has lobular carcinoma in situ.

248
00:15:51,410 --> 00:15:55,190
Now, one of the key features about lobular carcinoma in situ is that when you look at the cells,

249
00:15:55,250 --> 00:15:59,450
let's pretend that this is the terminal duct and this is the lobular unit, and you've got

250
00:15:59,450 --> 00:16:04,050
these glands here, when you look at the cells, the cells are often discohesive, which means

251
00:16:04,050 --> 00:16:05,530
they are not stuck to one another.

252
00:16:05,670 --> 00:16:07,830
Instead, they're very separate from one another.

253
00:16:08,410 --> 00:16:12,550
And that's important because one of the key characteristics of this particular tumor is

254
00:16:12,550 --> 00:16:16,150
that it lacks an adhesion molecule called ecadherin.

255
00:16:16,470 --> 00:16:20,070
Now, ecadherin is the glue that allows cells to stick together.

256
00:16:20,650 --> 00:16:22,470
It's a cellular adhesion molecule.

257
00:16:22,470 --> 00:16:25,710
It's glue that allows these cells to stick to one another.

258
00:16:25,710 --> 00:16:30,530
So, if lobular carcinoma in situ is lacking ecadherin, it wouldn't be hard to understand

259
00:16:30,530 --> 00:16:35,110
why the cells often appear discohesive or not attached to one another.

260
00:16:35,550 --> 00:16:40,430
This is particularly high yield concerning lobular carcinoma, that it lacks ecadherin due

261
00:16:40,430 --> 00:16:41,930
to a mutation in that gene.

262
00:16:42,570 --> 00:16:47,890
Now, often, lobular carcinoma in situ is actually multifocal and bilateral, so that if a patient

263
00:16:47,890 --> 00:16:52,430
has lobular carcinoma, for example, here in the breast, it'll also be here and here

264
00:16:52,430 --> 00:16:58,550
often, and it would not be uncommon to also find it in the other breast in various locations.

265
00:16:59,110 --> 00:17:02,890
So, lobular carcinoma in situ is often multifocal and bilateral.

266
00:17:04,070 --> 00:17:09,650
Now, a current understanding of lobular carcinoma in situ is that it is a risk factor for the

267
00:17:09,650 --> 00:17:11,210
future development of breast cancer.

268
00:17:11,650 --> 00:17:15,030
So, we don't try to excise lobular carcinoma in situ.

269
00:17:15,030 --> 00:17:20,350
Instead, we use pharmacologic agents such as tamoxifen, which is an anti-estrogen agent,

270
00:17:20,870 --> 00:17:27,130
with the hopes of decreasing the risk of those cells actually progressing to eventually become

271
00:17:27,130 --> 00:17:28,570
invasive carcinoma.

272
00:17:29,210 --> 00:17:31,910
And then, in addition, we would give close follow-up.

273
00:17:32,330 --> 00:17:37,930
So, our current understanding of lobular carcinoma in situ is that it's more of a risk factor

274
00:17:37,930 --> 00:17:41,170
than it is a quote-unquote malignant proliferation.

275
00:17:41,170 --> 00:17:46,110
So, generally speaking, with lobular carcinoma in situ, there's a low risk of progression

276
00:17:46,110 --> 00:17:47,590
to invasive carcinoma.

277
00:17:48,350 --> 00:17:53,910
Invasive lobular carcinoma is a tumor of lobular cells that invades beyond the basement membrane

278
00:17:53,910 --> 00:17:56,670
into the actual connective tissue of the breast.

279
00:17:57,130 --> 00:18:00,910
Now, one of the interesting things is that, remember, in ductal carcinoma in situ, the

280
00:18:00,910 --> 00:18:02,870
cells produce ductal-like structures.

281
00:18:03,710 --> 00:18:09,290
However, in invasive lobular carcinoma, the cells classically invade in a single-file pattern.

282
00:18:09,290 --> 00:18:11,790
The cells are all dispersed and single from one another.

283
00:18:12,790 --> 00:18:16,150
The reason why this occurs is because the tumor cells lack E-cadherin.

284
00:18:16,530 --> 00:18:21,670
Remember that E-cadherin is a cellular adhesion molecule that allows cells to stick together.

285
00:18:21,930 --> 00:18:24,610
So, that would then allow those cells to make ducts.

286
00:18:24,910 --> 00:18:28,290
However, in the case of invasive lobular carcinoma, there is no E-cadherin.

287
00:18:28,630 --> 00:18:34,230
Therefore, the cells invade singly, quote-unquote, in a classical single-file pattern.

288
00:18:34,650 --> 00:18:35,710
And that's super high yield.

289
00:18:35,950 --> 00:18:37,230
Super high yield to know why.

290
00:18:37,230 --> 00:18:38,730
E-cadherin mutations.

291
00:18:39,290 --> 00:18:42,930
Here's a classic picture, and you can see that there's these tumor cells, which are invading

292
00:18:42,930 --> 00:18:47,330
singly in a single-file pattern, and not really producing ductal structures.

293
00:18:48,230 --> 00:18:51,770
Now, generally speaking, when we have a patient that has breast cancer, the next thing the

294
00:18:51,770 --> 00:18:54,650
patient's going to want to know is, what's my chance of survival?

295
00:18:56,090 --> 00:19:00,730
Remember that the main way by which we determine prognosis is the TNM staging system.

296
00:19:00,730 --> 00:19:04,850
Now, the T, again, stands for tumor, and that's based on the size of the tumor.

297
00:19:05,210 --> 00:19:10,250
The N stands for metastasis to local lymph nodes, and so we would assess the axillary

298
00:19:10,250 --> 00:19:12,470
lymph nodes to determine the N stage.

299
00:19:12,830 --> 00:19:15,090
And then the M refers to distant metastasis.

300
00:19:15,630 --> 00:19:19,050
Now, metastasis is the most important prognostic factor.

301
00:19:19,050 --> 00:19:24,650
If a patient has metastasis outside of the region, outside of the breast and the axillary lymph

302
00:19:24,650 --> 00:19:28,350
nodes, that would be a very poor prognostic sign.

303
00:19:29,130 --> 00:19:33,690
Although metastasis is the most important factor, however, most patients don't usually

304
00:19:33,690 --> 00:19:34,950
present with metastasis.

305
00:19:35,470 --> 00:19:39,390
So, therefore, the most useful factor becomes spread to axillary lymph nodes.

306
00:19:39,450 --> 00:19:41,730
And I hope that's clear, because that's particularly high yield.

307
00:19:41,730 --> 00:19:43,470
I'm going to repeat it one more time.

308
00:19:43,990 --> 00:19:45,590
Metastasis is most important.

309
00:19:45,970 --> 00:19:50,790
However, because most patients don't present with metastasis, what becomes particularly useful

310
00:19:50,790 --> 00:19:55,490
in those patients is the second most important factor, which is spread to the axillary lymph

311
00:19:55,490 --> 00:19:55,750
nodes.

312
00:19:56,570 --> 00:20:00,070
The way by which we determine spread to the axillary lymph nodes is called the sentinel

313
00:20:00,070 --> 00:20:01,050
lymph node biopsy.

314
00:20:01,430 --> 00:20:03,470
So, let me just highlight the principle here.

315
00:20:03,990 --> 00:20:07,990
Basically, if a patient has a tumor within the breast, let's say that this is a cancer within

316
00:20:07,990 --> 00:20:11,710
the breast, our understanding is that that breast would then drain to the axillary lymph

317
00:20:11,730 --> 00:20:16,850
nodes, and that we would like to examine the axillary lymph nodes to determine whether

318
00:20:16,850 --> 00:20:20,390
there's cancer in those nodes, because it affects whether we should, for example, give

319
00:20:20,390 --> 00:20:22,070
the patient chemotherapy or not.

320
00:20:22,710 --> 00:20:27,090
Now, when we have this cancer within the breast, and we'd like to assess the axilla, one way

321
00:20:27,090 --> 00:20:29,630
to do it would be to remove all of the axillary lymph nodes.

322
00:20:30,110 --> 00:20:35,370
However, the problem with that is that if the patient has no cancer in the lymph nodes,

323
00:20:35,450 --> 00:20:39,310
then we've removed the lymph nodes, and now the patient has no draining from the

324
00:20:39,310 --> 00:20:42,430
arm, and therefore would develop swelling of the arm as a complication.

325
00:20:43,250 --> 00:20:48,350
It's an acceptable complication if the patient has cancer, but the reality is that 75% of

326
00:20:48,350 --> 00:20:53,150
patients who have breast cancer don't even have cancer in the lymph nodes, and so in 75%

327
00:20:53,150 --> 00:20:56,230
of our patients, we were causing arm swelling when we didn't really need to.

328
00:20:56,730 --> 00:21:01,010
So, someone came up with a brilliant idea called the sentinel lymph node biopsy, where the notion

329
00:21:01,010 --> 00:21:07,150
is that you inject the breast with either a blue dye or a radioactive substance, and allow

330
00:21:07,150 --> 00:21:10,950
that blue dye or radioactive substance to go to the lymph nodes.

331
00:21:11,310 --> 00:21:15,190
Now, it so happens that the lymph nodes in the breast are organized in tiers, so that you

332
00:21:15,190 --> 00:21:19,010
kind of have a line of lymph nodes, and then another line of lymph nodes, and another line

333
00:21:19,010 --> 00:21:20,230
of lymph nodes behind that.

334
00:21:21,210 --> 00:21:26,190
So, the goal in the sentinel lymph node biopsy would be to inject dye or radioactive agent,

335
00:21:26,190 --> 00:21:33,050
and then allow that dye to assess the very first line of lymph nodes that is being drained

336
00:21:33,050 --> 00:21:33,670
by the breast.

337
00:21:33,670 --> 00:21:38,370
Once you've marked those lymph nodes with the blue dye or the radio tracer, you open up

338
00:21:38,370 --> 00:21:40,470
the axilla, and you remove those lymph nodes.

339
00:21:40,830 --> 00:21:44,670
If those lymph nodes are negative, you can make the assumption that the rest of the lymph

340
00:21:44,670 --> 00:21:48,690
nodes behind those nodes are also negative, whereas if these lymph nodes are positive,

341
00:21:49,050 --> 00:21:52,850
you'll go back and remove, at that point in time, all of the other lymph nodes.

342
00:21:53,270 --> 00:21:58,030
So, that's called the sentinel lymph node biopsy, and it allows us to take minimal number of

343
00:21:58,030 --> 00:22:01,550
lymph nodes while still accurately staging the patient.

344
00:22:03,670 --> 00:22:08,370
So, that's a good paradigm to be aware of when thinking about prognosis in breast cancer.

345
00:22:08,370 --> 00:22:12,470
Now, we can also determine some predictive factors when dealing with breast cancer.

346
00:22:12,470 --> 00:22:15,950
And predictive factors predict response to treatment.

347
00:22:15,950 --> 00:22:18,490
That's the purpose of determining predictive factors.

348
00:22:18,490 --> 00:22:22,570
And there are three key predictive factors that we need to be aware of.

349
00:22:22,570 --> 00:22:27,770
The first is ER, the second is PR, and the third is HER2 new gene amplification.

350
00:22:27,770 --> 00:22:33,950
Now, basically, the notion is that if the tumor cells express estrogen receptor and the tumor

351
00:22:33,950 --> 00:22:38,690
cells express progesterone receptor, then that would mean that we could potentially expect

352
00:22:38,690 --> 00:22:42,410
a response when we gave them anti-estrogenic agents, like tamoxifen.

353
00:22:42,410 --> 00:22:48,470
And so, we will take a patient's tumor cells and test whether they express estrogen receptor

354
00:22:48,470 --> 00:22:49,890
and progesterone receptor.

355
00:22:50,790 --> 00:22:55,710
And these are two very important tests we do to predict response to anti-estrogenic agents,

356
00:22:55,790 --> 00:22:56,910
such as tamoxifen.

357
00:22:57,670 --> 00:23:00,010
And so, this would be two of the important tests.

358
00:23:00,370 --> 00:23:03,490
The third is amplification of the HER2 new gene.

359
00:23:03,830 --> 00:23:09,270
Now, remember that the HER2 new, I'm going to remind you, going back to our neoplasia lecture,

360
00:23:09,270 --> 00:23:13,330
HER2 new is a cell surface growth factor receptor.

361
00:23:13,870 --> 00:23:19,650
And if the patient has excess HER2 new, classically due to gene amplification,

362
00:23:19,930 --> 00:23:22,110
that's important, it's due to gene amplification,

363
00:23:22,630 --> 00:23:25,650
that excess receptor can result in excess growth of the cell,

364
00:23:25,910 --> 00:23:28,570
which can then drive the proliferation of the tumor.

365
00:23:29,070 --> 00:23:34,030
And so, this is an important predictive factor because we have antibodies that we've designed

366
00:23:34,030 --> 00:23:36,590
called HERceptin or trastuzumab.

367
00:23:36,590 --> 00:23:38,950
Now, we have antibodies against those receptors,

368
00:23:39,150 --> 00:23:42,650
and patients who have excess receptor can be treated with this antibody.

369
00:23:43,210 --> 00:23:47,050
Here's a couple stains that I've provided to show you how we actually do this.

370
00:23:47,330 --> 00:23:51,590
So, when we have a breast cancer, we can do immunohistochemical stains for estrogen receptor,

371
00:23:51,750 --> 00:23:52,970
as I'm showing here.

372
00:23:53,330 --> 00:23:57,450
And, of course, the estrogen receptor complex is present in the nucleus.

373
00:23:57,570 --> 00:24:00,710
Now, remember that the receptor itself is present in the cytoplasm,

374
00:24:01,090 --> 00:24:05,750
but when it binds its partner, it then moves to the nucleus.

375
00:24:05,750 --> 00:24:09,710
And so, you can clearly see that the nuclei of all these cells are brown,

376
00:24:10,110 --> 00:24:12,990
indicating that the tumor is positive for estrogen receptor.

377
00:24:13,490 --> 00:24:15,850
Now, progesterone receptor would look exactly the same

378
00:24:15,850 --> 00:24:20,190
because it is also a receptor that translocates itself to the nucleus.

379
00:24:20,950 --> 00:24:23,490
HER2 new, on the other hand, would not be present in the nucleus,

380
00:24:23,650 --> 00:24:26,690
but is present on the surface of the cells on the cytoplasm.

381
00:24:26,990 --> 00:24:30,430
And I've already explained that HER2 new is a growth factor receptor

382
00:24:30,430 --> 00:24:32,890
that would be seen on the surface of the cells.

383
00:24:35,750 --> 00:24:39,410
Now, a slide, which is that HER2 new is a cell surface receptor.

384
00:24:39,550 --> 00:24:43,290
Remember that it's a proto-oncogene that, when amplified, becomes an oncogene.

385
00:24:44,310 --> 00:24:48,290
Now, when you think about all of these three key predictive factors,

386
00:24:48,550 --> 00:24:51,410
that can create a wide variety of possibilities.

387
00:24:51,670 --> 00:24:56,190
For example, if you're thinking about ER and then PR and then HER2 new,

388
00:24:56,190 --> 00:25:01,870
it's possible that some patients could have ER positive, PR positive, HER2 negative tumors.

389
00:25:02,330 --> 00:25:06,270
It's also possible that you could be ER positive, PR negative, HER2 negative.

390
00:25:06,810 --> 00:25:12,990
It's possible to be negative for ER, negative for PR, and positive for HER2, etc., etc.

391
00:25:13,190 --> 00:25:15,610
But there's one particular subtype to be aware of,

392
00:25:15,610 --> 00:25:18,190
and that's when patients are negative, negative, negative,

393
00:25:18,190 --> 00:25:20,530
negative for all three predictive markers.

394
00:25:20,530 --> 00:25:23,350
And in those cases, patients are called triple negative.

395
00:25:23,810 --> 00:25:26,850
What's important about this subtype is that it has a poor prognosis,

396
00:25:27,350 --> 00:25:30,710
and that also African-American women have an increased propensity

397
00:25:30,710 --> 00:25:32,910
to develop triple negative carcinoma.

398
00:25:33,890 --> 00:25:37,530
Hereditary breast cancer represents 10% of cases of breast cancer,

399
00:25:37,890 --> 00:25:40,410
and there are some features that you can see within a patient

400
00:25:40,410 --> 00:25:42,550
that would suggest hereditary breast cancer.

401
00:25:42,930 --> 00:25:46,790
For example, if the patient has multiple first-degree relatives with breast cancer,

402
00:25:46,790 --> 00:25:51,050
you might think about the fact that the patient may have hereditary breast cancer.

403
00:25:51,550 --> 00:25:54,010
If the patient has tumor at a premenopausal age,

404
00:25:54,310 --> 00:25:57,910
remember that breast cancer is classically a disease of postmenopausal females.

405
00:25:58,410 --> 00:26:01,050
However, if the patient gets cancer at a premenopausal age,

406
00:26:01,310 --> 00:26:04,330
you might think about testing the patient for hereditary breast cancer.

407
00:26:04,750 --> 00:26:07,950
And also, if a patient develops multiple tumors, that would be highly unlikely,

408
00:26:08,730 --> 00:26:11,030
and so again, we would test for hereditary breast cancer.

409
00:26:11,690 --> 00:26:13,570
When considering hereditary breast cancer,

410
00:26:13,570 --> 00:26:18,910
BRCA1 and BRCA2 are currently the two most important single-gene mutations

411
00:26:18,910 --> 00:26:22,330
in the domain of hereditary breast cancer.

412
00:26:22,690 --> 00:26:25,850
Let's remind ourselves that BRCA1 patients have an increased risk

413
00:26:25,850 --> 00:26:28,450
for both breast and ovarian carcinoma,

414
00:26:28,630 --> 00:26:31,010
and I'd like to remind you of a couple high-yield points here.

415
00:26:31,390 --> 00:26:33,910
Number one, breast cancer, there's an increased propensity

416
00:26:33,910 --> 00:26:35,930
for medullary carcinoma of the breast.

417
00:26:36,310 --> 00:26:38,630
Remember that discussion that we just had a few moments ago.

418
00:26:38,630 --> 00:26:43,270
Now, medullary carcinoma is not the most common type of breast cancer

419
00:26:43,270 --> 00:26:44,590
in these particular individuals.

420
00:26:45,110 --> 00:26:51,590
However, there is an increased propensity compared to the normal population.

421
00:26:52,090 --> 00:26:55,690
And then the second is to remind you that when you consider the ovarian carcinoma

422
00:26:55,690 --> 00:26:59,790
in the BRCA1 patients, it's classically a serous carcinoma,

423
00:27:00,010 --> 00:27:01,890
and I'd like to very clearly remind you

424
00:27:01,890 --> 00:27:04,750
that it doesn't only increase the risk for ovarian carcinoma,

425
00:27:04,750 --> 00:27:07,050
but this can also arise within the fallopian tube,

426
00:27:07,470 --> 00:27:10,090
and that's a high-yield principle that I talked about in your gynae lectures.

427
00:27:10,710 --> 00:27:15,190
When you think about BRCA2, it's classically associated with breast cancer in males.

428
00:27:15,350 --> 00:27:18,010
That's one of the classic associations with BRCA2.

429
00:27:18,450 --> 00:27:21,950
Now, I'd like to remind you, when patients have a hereditary breast cancer,

430
00:27:22,390 --> 00:27:26,050
they may elect to undergo prophylactic mastectomy.

431
00:27:26,230 --> 00:27:30,570
So, for example, if a woman learns that she's got a BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutation,

432
00:27:31,290 --> 00:27:33,670
she may choose to actually have both breasts removed.

433
00:27:33,670 --> 00:27:36,290
If both breasts are removed, however,

434
00:27:36,590 --> 00:27:39,590
it's very important to note that a small risk still remains

435
00:27:39,590 --> 00:27:41,130
for the development of breast cancer,

436
00:27:41,490 --> 00:27:44,030
and that's because sometimes we have excess breast tissue

437
00:27:44,030 --> 00:27:46,490
that can go, for example, into the muscle

438
00:27:46,490 --> 00:27:49,130
or may be present up in the axilla, etc.

439
00:27:49,710 --> 00:27:55,370
And so, although prophylactic mastectomy sharply would reduce the risk

440
00:27:55,370 --> 00:27:58,650
of future development of cancer, the risk does not become zero,

441
00:27:58,790 --> 00:28:01,550
and those patients still need to be watched carefully.

442
00:28:01,550 --> 00:28:05,450
Male breast cancer is the last discussion that we want to have,

443
00:28:05,570 --> 00:28:07,910
and all I'd like to say about this is, number one, it's rare.

444
00:28:08,270 --> 00:28:12,590
Remember that for every 100 cases of breast cancer, only one will involve a male,

445
00:28:12,870 --> 00:28:14,710
whereas 99 will be seen in a female.

446
00:28:15,450 --> 00:28:17,730
It presents as a subareolar mast.

447
00:28:17,950 --> 00:28:21,470
That's to be expected because we know that the vast majority of breast tissue

448
00:28:21,470 --> 00:28:24,490
is in the subareolar region in a male,

449
00:28:24,810 --> 00:28:27,130
whereas, again, the vast majority of breast tissue in a female

450
00:28:27,130 --> 00:28:28,670
is in the upper outer quadrant.

451
00:28:29,270 --> 00:28:31,650
So, usually it's going to be in an older male,

452
00:28:31,950 --> 00:28:33,490
and it can produce nipple discharge,

453
00:28:33,590 --> 00:28:35,090
and the reason it produces nipple discharge

454
00:28:35,090 --> 00:28:38,410
is because the tumor is developing right underneath the nipple.

455
00:28:39,350 --> 00:28:41,670
Now, most often, patients who have male breast cancer

456
00:28:41,670 --> 00:28:43,630
will develop a ductal carcinoma,

457
00:28:43,970 --> 00:28:45,790
and the idea here goes back to embryology,

458
00:28:45,890 --> 00:28:47,510
and that is that when you've got a male breast,

459
00:28:47,570 --> 00:28:48,990
you do develop terminal ducts,

460
00:28:49,250 --> 00:28:51,230
but you don't yet develop those lobules.

461
00:28:51,230 --> 00:28:55,450
And so, most of the breast tissue in males is actually ducts

462
00:28:55,450 --> 00:28:56,590
with very few lobules,

463
00:28:56,990 --> 00:28:59,650
and so they usually develop invasive ductal carcinoma.

464
00:29:00,070 --> 00:29:03,070
And the last point is that there's an association with BRCA2 mutation,

465
00:29:03,210 --> 00:29:04,950
as I just stated a moment ago,

466
00:29:05,130 --> 00:29:08,090
and there's also an increased risk in patients with Klinefelter syndrome.

467
00:29:08,670 --> 00:29:10,950
So, this concludes our discussion of breast cancer,

468
00:29:11,110 --> 00:29:13,170
and now we can move on to the next chapter.

